WEBVTT

1
00:00:03.400 --> 00:00:06.200
How do you tell how a cell died?

2
00:00:07.440 --> 00:00:08.520
Looking is not enough.

3
00:00:09.120 --> 00:00:14.480
A dying cell's appearance names where it ended up, not what killed it.

4
00:00:15.040 --> 00:00:17.440
So we ask each cell two questions.

5
00:00:18.080 --> 00:00:19.600
One is about a lipid.

6
00:00:20.220 --> 00:00:21.820
The other is about the membrane.

7
00:00:22.833 --> 00:00:28.753
A healthy cell keeps a lipid called phosphatidylserine on the inner face of its

8
00:00:28.753 --> 00:00:34.833
plasma membrane. Annexin V is a protein that binds this lipid, with calcium.

9
00:00:35.773 --> 00:00:38.233
From outside, it cannot reach the lipid.

10
00:00:39.533 --> 00:00:41.833
Propidium iodide is a DNA dye.

11
00:00:42.873 --> 00:00:43.873
Live cells keep it out.

12
00:00:44.583 --> 00:00:47.543
Now the cell dies by apoptosis.

13
00:00:48.143 --> 00:00:50.663
The lipid turns up on the outer face.

14
00:00:51.363 --> 00:00:53.543
Annexin V can reach it and bind.

15
00:00:54.303 --> 00:00:57.563
That binding means the lipid is reachable from outside.

16
00:00:58.363 --> 00:01:02.663
But the membrane still holds, so propidium iodide stays out.

17
00:01:03.383 --> 00:01:07.123
This cell is annexin V positive and PI negative.

18
00:01:07.643 --> 00:01:09.863
It is the clearest apoptotic signal.

19
00:01:10.617 --> 00:01:12.097
Then comes a leak.

20
00:01:12.937 --> 00:01:18.837
In culture, with nothing to eat it, an apoptotic cell generally becomes leaky.

21
00:01:19.157 --> 00:01:21.277
This is called secondary necrosis.

22
00:01:22.497 --> 00:01:25.477
Propidium iodide enters and stains the DNA.

23
00:01:26.437 --> 00:01:29.877
Its entry means the membrane is leaky, whatever made it so.

24
00:01:30.777 --> 00:01:32.737
Now the cell is positive for both.

25
00:01:33.650 --> 00:01:35.050
Now read the plot.

26
00:01:35.850 --> 00:01:41.630
In this made-up data set, a flow cytometer reads 10,000 Jurkat cells after six

27
00:01:41.630 --> 00:01:45.830
hours of drug. Each cell lands in one of four boxes.

28
00:01:46.730 --> 00:01:49.130
5,800 are negative for both.

29
00:01:49.790 --> 00:01:53.010
2,900 are annexin V positive only.

30
00:01:53.610 --> 00:01:55.490
1,000 are positive for both.

31
00:01:56.750 --> 00:01:59.330
300 are positive for propidium iodide only.

32
00:01:59.910 --> 00:02:02.210
A double positive cell is a puzzle.

33
00:02:02.210 --> 00:02:07.530
It could be a late apoptotic cell, or a cell that died by necrosis from the start.

34
00:02:08.230 --> 00:02:10.230
One time point cannot tell them apart.

35
00:02:11.050 --> 00:02:13.370
So follow the cells in time.

36
00:02:14.130 --> 00:02:19.890
An apoptotic cell exposes the lipid first, and stays PI negative for a while.

37
00:02:20.590 --> 00:02:25.670
It should pass through the annexin V positive box before it turns double positive.

38
00:02:26.610 --> 00:02:32.350
A time course helps but cannot settle it alone: necroptotic cells can pass through

39
00:02:32.350 --> 00:02:34.890
that box too. Keep the rule.

40
00:02:35.550 --> 00:02:37.550
One marker reports one event.

41
00:02:37.550 --> 00:02:40.710
Combine markers, and follow them in time.
